{"count": 26535, "next": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=889", "previous": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=887", "results": [{"id": 76605, "name": "Merle Oberon", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2475698, "title": "Wuthering Heights", "work_id": 80109, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2293120, "title": "These three", "work_id": 85357, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1911-02-19", "date_of_death": "1979-11-23", "genders": [{"id": 5, "name": "Female", "slug": "female"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 10, "name": "United Kingdom", "slug": "united-kingdom"}], "biography": "Merle Oberon (born Estelle Merle O'Brien Thompson; 19 February 1911 – 23 November 1979) was a British actress who began her film career in British films as Anne Boleyn in The Private Life of Henry VIII (1933). After her success in The Scarlet Pimpernel (1934), she travelled to the United States to make films for Samuel Goldwyn. She was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Actress for her performance in The Dark Angel (1935). Oberon hid her mixed heritage out of fear of discrimination and the impact it would have had on her career.She was perhaps best known for her portrayal of Catherine Earnshaw in the 1939 film adaptation of the novel Wuthering Heights directed by William Wyler.\nHer other notable films are These Three (1936), A Song to Remember (1945), Berlin Express (1948) and Désirée (1954).\nA traffic collision in 1937 caused facial injuries that could have ended her career, but she recovered and remained active in film and television until 1973.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-09-05T23:01:17.287022+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 4673, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merle_Oberon"}], "uuid": "13019de0-9589-428e-ba15-1952c679729c", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "38147", "external_references": [{"id": 26888, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q230958"}, {"id": 30271, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "14972204"}, {"id": 30272, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n78073879"}, {"id": 30273, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n78073879"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-05T23:01:19.953908+10:00"}, {"id": 76604, "name": "David Niven Jr", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2475697, "title": "Wuthering Heights", "work_id": 80109, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2345408, "title": "The Eagle has landed [Widescreen]", "work_id": 93981, "role": "co-producer", "role_id": 915, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 2325187, "title": "That's dancing!", "work_id": 98924, "role": "producer", "role_id": 1089, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "", "date_of_death": "", "genders": [], "languages": [], "places_of_operation": [], "biography": "", "biography_author": null, "date_of_biography": "2023-08-17T15:11:35.390662+10:00", "external_links": [], "uuid": "a1a1e0b9-5ae9-4a8a-aff4-8334492cc03d", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "17736", "external_references": [], "date_modified": "2023-08-17T15:11:35.390873+10:00"}, {"id": 76603, "name": "Alfred Newman", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 1985254, "title": "How the West was won", "work_id": 81186, "role": "co-producer", "role_id": 915, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 2475695, "title": "Wuthering Heights", "work_id": 80109, "role": "music", "role_id": 949, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2107182, "title": "Ball of fire", "work_id": 84667, "role": "music", "role_id": 949, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2351963, "title": "The Grapes of Wrath", "work_id": 80788, "role": "music", "role_id": 949, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 802950, "title": "Nana", "work_id": 89523, "role": "music", "role_id": 949, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1901-03-17", "date_of_death": "1970-02-17", "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Alfred Newman (March 17, 1900 – February 17, 1970) was an American composer, arranger, and conductor of film music. From his start as a music prodigy, he came to be regarded as a respected figure in the history of film music. He won nine Academy Awards and was nominated 45 times, contributing to the extended Newman family being the most Academy Award-nominated family, with a collective 92 nominations in various music categories.\nIn a career spanning more than four decades, Newman composed the scores for over 200 motion pictures. Some of his most famous scores include Wuthering Heights, The Hunchback of Notre Dame, The Mark of Zorro, How Green Was My Valley, The Song of Bernadette, Captain from Castile, All About Eve, Love is a Many Splendored Thing, Anastasia, The Diary of Anne Frank, How The West Was Won, The Greatest Story Ever Told, and his final score, Airport, all of which were nominated for or won Academy Awards. He is perhaps best known for composing the fanfare which accompanies the studio logo at the beginning of 20th Century Fox's productions. Prior to commencing his employment with 20th Century Fox, Newman composed the fanfares which are most often associated with Samuel Goldwyn productions and David O. Selznick productions.\nNewman was also highly regarded as a conductor, and arranged and conducted many scores by other composers, including George Gershwin, Charlie Chaplin, and Irving Berlin. He also conducted the music for many film adaptations of Broadway musicals (having worked on Broadway for ten years before coming to Hollywood), as well as many original Hollywood musicals.\nHe was among the first musicians to compose and conduct original music during Hollywood's Golden Age of movies, later becoming a respected and powerful music director in the history of Hollywood. Newman and two of his fellow composers, Max Steiner and Dimitri Tiomkin, were considered the \"three godfathers of film music\".", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-09-05T17:28:09.154763+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 3526, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Newman"}, {"id": 1608, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Newman_(composer)"}], "uuid": "4b349fbc-4b84-4bd9-bb69-9bf0b5f81d00", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "14958", "external_references": [{"id": 7035, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q367032"}, {"id": 7036, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "74038460"}, {"id": 7037, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n85376784"}, {"id": 7038, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n85376784"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-05T17:28:11.209564+10:00"}, {"id": 76602, "name": "Robin de Crespigny", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 771493, "title": "This time, next time", "work_id": 80108, "role": "producer/director", "role_id": 31, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": null, "date_of_death": null, "genders": [], "languages": [], "places_of_operation": [], "biography": "", "biography_author": null, "date_of_biography": null, "external_links": [], "uuid": "61262ece-3862-4f47-be06-0c5e88624a2f", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": null, "external_references": [{"id": 51509, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q121360557"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-06T00:49:15.363166+10:00"}, {"id": 76601, "name": "Ches Yetman", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 912833, "title": "Ikwe", "work_id": 92928, "role": "co-producer", "role_id": 9